{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md | sed -n '75,105p;180,230p;265,290p'\"","aggregated_output":"    75\t`(dao.jing.dht/step state budget) -> state'` is the only thing that advances\n    76\tthe DHT. One owner per state drives it, at the composition's cadence. The\n    77\tstate is a plain map: handles, cursors, the routing table, and the pending\n    78\ttables.\n    79\t\n    80\t**Solo mode** is a composition with no `:traffic` and no `:datagrams`. It\n    81\topens no socket, holds no pending network work, and answers as section 4.3\n    82\tand 4.5 say. Configuring peers later is a new composition.\n    83\t\n    84\tOne step, in this order, each stage bounded by `budget`:\n    85\t\n    86\t1. Drain `:ticks` to `blocked`; the newest reading is `now`.\n    87\t2. Read `:traffic`: validate each datagram (section 7, 8), deliver replies to\n    88\t   pending queries, serve requests.\n    89\t3. Read `:requests`.\n    90\t4. Advance pending work: expire deadlines against `now`, issue queries,\n    91\t   retries and sends.\n    92\t5. Append answers and facts.\n    93\t\n    94\tA `gap` on `:requests`, `:traffic` or `:ticks` adopts the recovery cursor and\n    95\tappends `{:dao.jing.dht/fact :dao.jing.dht/gap :dao.jing.dht/stream s}`. A\n    96\trequest lost to a gap is never answered; its asker re-asks. Every operation\n    97\there is idempotent by content address, so re-asking is always safe.\n    98\t\n    99\t## 3. The stream API\n   100\t\n   101\t**Requests and answers are the `dao.jing.content` convention**\n   102\t(`src/cljc/dao/jing/content.cljc`), unchanged and exact. The DHT is one more\n   103\tserving half of that convention, a sibling of `serve-step` whose answers may\n   104\tarrive on a later step. The clients are the existing ones:\n   105\t`dao.jing.content.step` (portable, stepped), `dao.jing.content.driver` (JVM\n   180\t- **The byte-store handle** (section 5.1). `put-bytes-fn` validates, inserts\n   181\t  into `:local`, appends `{:dao.jing.dht/replicate address}` to the request\n   182\t  stream, and returns the local verdict `:inserted` or `:present` at once.\n   183\t  The return value is the byte-store contract's local verdict. It is **not**\n   184\t  the acknowledgement; the acknowledgement is the `/sent` fact.\n   185\t- **A `:jing/put` request.** The step validates (the one ingress check,\n   186\t  `dao.jing/accept-bytes!`), inserts into `:local`, and holds the answer.\n   187\t  `{:jing/request r :jing/result verdict}` is appended when the write is\n   188\t  acknowledged, and in a DHT composition that answer **is** the\n   189\t  acknowledgement.\n   190\t\n   191\tBoth create or join one pending write per address. A pending write records\n   192\tthe address, the request ids waiting on it, the peers sent to, and its\n   193\tdeadline. It never holds the payload; bytes are read from `:local` when sent.\n   194\t\n   195\t### 4.3 How a write proceeds, and what the caller sees when it cannot\n   196\t\n   197\tA pending write never waits on a lookup to acknowledge:\n   198\t\n   199\t1. **Candidates** are the proven routing entries nearest the address's digest\n   200\t   that this node already knows: a table read, no IO.\n   201\t2. **Send** the store message to fresh candidates, nearest first, until `ack-peers` are counted. With a warm table this happens in the\n   202\t   step that read the request: the acknowledgement is immediate.\n   203\t3. **Too few known peers** (a cold bootstrap, a small table): the write starts\n   204\t   an iterative lookup toward the digest and pings candidates; each newly\n   205\t   proven peer is sent to as it appears.\n   206\t4. **Acknowledge** the moment the count reaches `ack-peers`: append the\n   207\t   `/sent` fact and answer every waiting `:jing/put`.\n   208\t5. **Continue after the acknowledgement**, best effort: finish the lookup and\n   209\t   send to the `k` nearest peers not already sent to, so that a reader's\n   210\t   lookup converges on holders. When done or at the deadline, append\n   211\t   `/replicated`. Nothing about the acknowledgement depends on this.\n   212\t\n   213\tIf the count has not reached `ack-peers` when `ack-ticks` have elapsed since\n   214\tthe write began, the write is **not acknowledged**: append the\n   215\t`/unacknowledged` fact and answer every waiting `:jing/put` with\n   216\t`{:jing/request r :jing/unacknowledged reason}`. The pending write is then\n   217\tforgotten. The local copy stays. Trying again is the caller's policy: append\n   218\tthe replicate request again.\n   219\t\n   220\t| Reason | When | Sent anything? |\n   221\t|---|---|---|\n   222\t| `:dao.jing.dht/solo` | No socket is composed. Answered in the step that reads the request. | No |\n   223\t| `:dao.jing.dht/unpublished` | `publish?` is false (section 4.4). Answered at once. | No |\n   224\t| `:dao.jing.dht/oversize` | The encoded store message exceeds `max-message-bytes`. Answered at once. | No |\n   225\t| `:dao.jing.dht/absent` | A replicate request names an address `:local` does not hold. | No |\n   226\t| `:dao.jing.dht/busy` | `max-pending-writes` is reached. Answered at once. | No |\n   227\t| `:dao.jing.dht/too-few-peers` | The deadline passed with fewer than `ack-peers` counted. `/peers m` says how many. | Possibly, to `m` |\n   228\t\n   229\tSo a caller always sees exactly one of `/sent` or `/unacknowledged` per\n   230\tpending write, unless the request itself was lost to a gap, which the `/gap`\n   265\t\n   266\t- Solo: answer not found, with a `/miss` fact of reason `/solo`.\n   267\t- Otherwise start a pending get: an iterative lookup toward the digest, asking\n   268\t  candidates with `:fetch` as they are proven, at most `alpha` outstanding.\n   269\t- A fetched payload is verified before anything else: strict decode, digest\n   270\t  against the requested address, canonical decode (`dao.jing/accept-bytes!`).\n   271\t  A failure is discarded and the next candidate tried.\n   272\t- A verified payload is inserted into `:local`, then answered found.\n   273\t- Candidates exhausted, or `get-ticks` elapsed: answer not found, with a\n   274\t  `/miss` fact.\n   275\t\n   276\t**Not found from a DHT is not authoritative absence.** It says no reachable\n   277\tcandidate produced a verifiable value in time.\n   278\t\n   279\t## 5. The `dao.jing` adapter, the JVM facade, and time\n   280\t\n   281\t### 5.1 The byte-store handle (owner decision 4)\n   282\t\n   283\t`(dao.jing.dht/store-handle {:local l :requests w :max-message-bytes n})` is\n   284\ta plain-data `dao.jing` handle, portable to every host:\n   285\t\n   286\t- `put-bytes-fn`: section 4.2. Local verdict, immediately.\n   287\t- `get-bytes-fn`: **`:local` only.** Bytes or the caller's sentinel. It never\n   288\t  waits, never returns a marker, and never touches the network.\n   289\t- `close-fn`: closes `:local`.\n   290\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
